# XSH setuid()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Appropriate privileges are implementation-defined (XBD 3.21): user ID 0
# does not prove them and a non-zero ID does not prove their absence. Each
# test runs in a child of a privileged test process and first probes, in
# a disposable process and the setup phase, a call that only a privileged
# process may make (setuid() to an ID the process does not hold); the test
# is UNRESOLVED when the probe does not show the state the assertion
# needs. User IDs used: 65534 and 65533 (given to the process), 65532
# (probes), 65531 (refused targets).

assertion  XSH.setuid.001
anchor     functions/setuid.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/setuid.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       With appropriate privileges, setuid(65534) returns 0 and sets
           the real, effective and saved set-user-ID to 65534: getuid() and
           geteuid() return 65534, and (no privilege for seteuid() left)
           seteuid(0) then fails with EPERM, so neither the real nor the
           saved set-user-ID is still 0.
needs      root
note       When the process keeps appropriate privileges after the call,
           the saved set-user-ID cannot be observed and the test is
           UNRESOLVED.
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/001-privileged.c

assertion  XSH.setuid.002
anchor     functions/setuid.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A process without appropriate privileges whose real and saved
           set-user-ID are 0 and effective user ID 65534 (seteuid(65534)
           as root) calls setuid(0): it returns 0 and geteuid() and
           getuid() return 0.
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/002-unprivileged-real.c

assertion  XSH.setuid.003
anchor     functions/setuid.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A process without appropriate privileges with real user ID
           65534 and effective and saved set-user-ID 65533 calls
           setuid(65534): it returns 0, both getuid() and geteuid() return
           65534, and seteuid(65533) still works (the saved set-user-ID is
           unchanged); setuid(65533) then returns 0 with geteuid() 65533 and
           getuid() 65534.
needs      root
note       setreuid() (XSI) sets the IDs apart, so the test runs only on
           XSI systems; the requirement itself is not XSI.
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/003-unprivileged-saved.c

assertion  XSH.setuid.004
anchor     functions/setuid.html#ERRORS/EPERM
anchor     functions/setuid.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       A process without appropriate privileges with real and saved
           set-user-ID 0 and effective user ID 65534 gets -1 with EPERM from
           setuid(65531) and from setuid(65534) (its effective ID, which is
           neither the real nor the saved one), and its IDs are unchanged.
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/004-eperm.c

assertion  XSH.setuid.005
anchor     functions/setuid.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.408
class      required
since      1988
says       With appropriate privileges, setuid((uid_t)-1) returns -1 with
           EINVAL ((uid_t)-1 is never a valid user ID) and the IDs stay 0.
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/005-einval.c

assertion  XSH.setuid.006
anchor     functions/setuid.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With appropriate privileges and the supplementary group list set
           to {65534}, setuid(65533) leaves the list getgroups() reports
           unchanged.
needs      root
note       The list is set with pc_drop_groups() (setgroups(), not in
           POSIX, where the system has it); without a supplementary group
           the test is UNRESOLVED.
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/006-groups.c

assertion  XSH.setuid.007
anchor     functions/setuid.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9
class      required
since      2001
says       When one thread of a privileged process calls setuid(65534),
           another thread of the process then gets 65534 from getuid() and
           geteuid() (the IDs belong to the process).
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/007-threads.c
